0. Deployment & DevOps (Salesforce – Production Grade)

This is where most developers fail interviews—not because they don’t know Apex, but because they don’t understand how code safely reaches production.

10.1 Deployment Methods
A. Change Sets (Declarative / Legacy but Still Used)
What it is

A UI-based deployment tool inside Salesforce used to move metadata between orgs (Sandbox → Production).

Types
Outbound Change Set → From Sandbox
Inbound Change Set → Into Target Org
How it works
Create Change Set in Sandbox
Add components (Apex, Objects, Flows, etc.)
Upload to target org
Validate → Deploy
Pros
No coding required
Safe for admins
Built-in dependency checker
Cons (IMPORTANT)
Not version-controlled
Manual & error-prone
No rollback
Not scalable for teams
When to use
Small orgs
Admin-driven changes
Quick fixes
B. Salesforce DX (Modern Standard)

Core tool: Salesforce DX

What it is

CLI-based development + deployment model using source-driven development

Key Concepts
1. Scratch Orgs
Temporary dev environments
Created via CLI
Fully configurable via JSON
sfdx force:org:create -f project-scratch-def.json
2. Source Format
Metadata stored as files (not zipped XML like old days)
Works with Git
3. CLI Deployment
sfdx force:source:deploy -p force-app
Pros
Version control friendly
Automation ready
Supports CI/CD
Team collaboration
Cons
Learning curve
Requires setup
When to use
Any serious team
Enterprise projects
CI/CD pipelines
C. Metadata API (Under the hood)

Used by:

Change Sets
SFDX
Tools like Workbench
Deployment Command Example
sfdx force:mdapi:deploy -d mdapi_output -w 10
10.2 Version Control (Git – Mandatory)

Core tool: Git

If you’re not using Git → you are not production-ready.

A. Why Git in Salesforce?
Track changes
Collaboration
Rollback capability
CI/CD integration
B. Basic Workflow
git init
git add .
git commit -m "Initial commit"
C. Branching Strategy (Production Standard)
1. Main Branches
main → Production
develop → Integration
2. Supporting Branches
feature/*
release/*
hotfix/*
Example Flow
feature/login → develop → release/v1 → main
D. Best Practices
One feature per branch
Small commits
Meaningful commit messages
Pull Request reviews mandatory
10.3 CI/CD (Critical for Real Companies)

CI/CD = Continuous Integration + Continuous Deployment

A. What is CI/CD?

Automated pipeline that:

Pulls code from Git
Runs tests
Validates deployment
Deploys automatically
B. Popular Tools
GitHub Actions
Jenkins
GitLab CI/CD
C. Typical Pipeline Flow
Developer pushes code → GitHub
        ↓
Pipeline triggers
        ↓
Run Apex Tests
        ↓
Static Code Analysis
        ↓
Deploy to Sandbox
        ↓
UAT Approval
        ↓
Deploy to Production
D. Example GitHub Actions Pipeline
name: Salesforce CI

on:
  push:
    branches: [develop]

jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    steps:
      - name: Checkout Code
        uses: actions/checkout@v2

      - name: Install Salesforce CLI
        run: npm install -g sfdx-cli

      - name: Authenticate Org
        run: sfdx auth:web:login

      - name: Deploy
        run: sfdx force:source:deploy -p force-app

      - name: Run Tests
        run: sfdx force:apex:test:run --resultformat human
E. Deployment Strategies
1. Validate First (MANDATORY)
sfdx force:source:deploy -c
Checks deployment without committing
2. Run Tests

Salesforce requirement:

Minimum 75% coverage
No failing tests
3. Quick Deploy
Use validation result to deploy instantly
